|> Section
    *Function signatures.* 
    In some real-world
    scenarios the inputs and outputs of a function
    come with certain units attached. In this case,
    we write
    !! the numbers fed to a function may come
    !! with a certain type of unit attached, and 
    !! those output by the function as well; in this context, we
    !! write

    $$
    \Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]
    $$

    to indicate that the 
    units of inputs to $f$ are A while the units
    of numbers output by $f$ are B. 
    !! former and latter units
    !! are respectively A and B.
    !! i.e., the above indicates that 
    !! $f$ is a function $f : \rr \ra \rr$
    !! that takes inputs with units A
    !! and produces outputs with units B. 
    For example,

    $$
    \Large h : [\te{kg}] \ra [^\circ\te{F}]
    $$

    would indicate that $h$ 
    is a function whose inputs denote kilograms and whose
    outputs denote degrees fahrenheit.
    We refer 
    to the ordered pair $(\te{A}, \te{B})$ as the

    __signature__

    of the function. Thus a
    function signature has
    two parts: the units of the input, and the units
    of the output!

|> Section
    *Dimensionless units.* There is a special unit
    that we denote

    $$
    \Large[1]
    $$

    that stands for a “dimensionless unit” or “dimensionless
    ratio” that, conceptually, arises when a quantity
    is divided by a like-dimensioned quantity. For example,

    __radians__

    are defined as “length over length” in the $xy$-plane—specifically,
    arc length divided by radius—and are one example
    of quantities that carry units of “$[1]$”.
    The outputs of sin, cos, etc, are other examples
    of dimensionless quantities,
    being ratios of the form
    “opposite over adjacent”, etc.
    Thus
    !! it would be correct to write

    $$
    \Large \cos : [1] \ra [1]
    $$

    !! (and likewise for any other trigonometric function)
    where the first ‘$[1]$’ 
    stands for a ratio of a curved length
    divided by a straight length—the radian—and the second
    ‘$[1]$’ stands for a ratio
    of one straight length by another*—the afore-mentioned
    “opposite over adjacent”. (Or “adjacent over hypotenuse”,
    in the case of cosine!)

    |> Pause

    _*Note._ To be precise, these “lengths” are actually
    coordinate changes that may be negative—except for the
    hypotenuse, which is always positive—so they
    are “signed lengths”, or _displacements_, more accurately. 
    Also the radian carries a sign—the radian says “rotate by
    this multiple of the radius, with ‘$+$’ for counterclockwise and ‘$-$’
    for clockwise\*\*”!

    !! |> Pause

    !! _\*\*Subnote #1._ Except for the hypotenuse, which is
    !! always nonnegative! (It's “opposite” and “adjacent” that
    !! change sign as you go around the circle, not “hypotenuse”.)

    |> Pause

    _\*\*Second Note._ To get into the weeds—re: clockwise
    vs. counterclockwise—what counts as the “positive” direction
    of rotation is actually the direction _from the positive $x$
    axis to the positive $y$ axis_:

    |> Pause

    What we mean is that if it was our custom to draw the
    $y$ axis pointing downwards, but still draw the $x$ axis
    pointing rightward, the “positive” direction of rotation
    would be clockwise, not counterclockwise:
    !! (This actually
    !! happens inside of certain drawing programs that deal with
    !! image formats for which the Y axis grows going downwards,
    !! such as SVG, in particular, the standard for so-called “vector graphics” in the web.)

    |> Pause

    (This actually happens inside of SVG, the web-based
    standard for so-called “vector graphics”, in which the $y$
    axis points downwards; in an SVG file, 
    |> span
        class=code-cartouche
        rotate(10)
    \ will rotate an object by 10$^\circ$ _clockwise_!)

|> Section
    *Fun fact. (“Fun.”)* 
    Imagine that we define the radian value of an
    angle between $0^\circ$ and $360^\circ$ as

    __length subtended by the angle on the unit circle__

    as opposed to

    __arc length over radius__

    which is the more “traditional” definition. In 
    the second 
    !! (“traditional”) 
    case, the dimensions of the
    radian are
    
    $$
    {\te{length}\over\te{length}} = \te{dimensionless}
    $$

    whereas under the first definition it sounds like
    the dimensions are simply “length”. BUT: “length”
    _in what plane_? The “original” plane in which the
    unit circle is embedded has dimensionless axes! And
    therefore _length itself_ in that plane is a dimensionless
    quantity, not the usual “physical” notion of length!
    So both cases assign radians to be a dimensionless 
    unit! Whoo-hoo!

    !! |> StarDivider

    !! Relatedly to the fact mentioned above, we will explain
    !! why some people use the following notation 

    !! |> Section
    !!     *Second (fun) fact.*
    !!     Hello.

    Relatedly to this idea of the original “vanilla”
    $xy$-plane having dimensionless axes, you may
    have seen people label axes this way, writing
    “time/s” instead of “time $[\te{s}]$”:
    !! or “weight/kg” instead of “weight $[\te{kg}]$”:

    |> Pause

    The idea behind this notation is that the plane
    itself is a pristine dimensionless object, so by doing

    $$
    \Large {\te{time}\over\te{s}}
    $$

    where “time” is a time measurements in seconds,
    we have “taken the units out of the measurement”
    and obtained a “pure number” that “fits” inside
    the plane; (!); for example, this ‘$3$’ here...

    |> Pause

    ...is really a
    “$3$ without any units”
    !! $$
    !! \Large 3[1]
    !! $$
    !! ...(“$3$ with dimensionless units”) 
    because the
    entire plane is dimensionless, but if we follow
    the semantics of the axis label, we find...

    $$
    \Large 3 = {\te{time}\over \te{s}}
    $$

    ...which implies...

    $$
    \Large 3\te{s} = \te{time}
    $$

    ...or “time equals $3$ seconds”, i.e., that vertical
    slice of the plane corresponds to the moment when
    the “time” variable—which _does_ have units—equals
    $3$ _seconds_, not just ‘$3$’, the pure number!

    (Nb: We will not be so artful in our labeling of axes,
    preferring the usual “time $[s]$” notation.)

|> Section

    *Function operations and signatures.*
    When we take the sum...

    $$
    \Large f + g
    $$

    ...of two functions, the same input, when evaluating the
    sum, is fed to both functions:

    $$
    \Large (f + g)(x) = f(x) + g(x).
    $$

    This means that in order for the sum to be make sense at
    all, the functions must take inputs with the same
    units; in addition, because apples should not be added
    to oranges, the two functions must output values with the
    same units as well; in other words, if $f$ and $g$ have
    signatures...

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{gathered}
    f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}] \\
    g : [\te{C}] \ra [\te{D}] \up{1.35}
    \end{gathered}
    $$

    ...then taking the sum...

    $$
    \Large f + g
    $$

    ...implies the constraints...

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{aligned}
    \te{A} &= \te{C} \\
    \te{B} &= \te{D} \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    ...on the units in order for the sum to be “well-formed”, not
    nonsensical!; i.e., the most general signature under which
    a sum of $f$ and $g$ makes sense is...

    $$
    \Large
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]& \\
    g : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]& \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    ...with the _same_ input units, and the _same_ output
    units!

|> Section
    handle=_ch6_start_

    *Notation.*
    We write

    $$
    \Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]
    $$

    to indicate that $f$ is a function $f : \rr \ra \rr$
    that takes inputs with units A
    and produces outputs with units B. 
    For example,

    $$
    \Large h : [\te{kg}] \ra [^\circ\te{F}]
    $$

    indicates that $h$ 
    is a function whose inputs denote kilograms and whose
    outputs denote degrees fahrenheit, etc.
    (These are purely “physical” concepts, not
    mathematical, but we're cool with that.)

    |> Pause

    *Vocabulary.* The input and output units of a function,
    when present as above, are referred to as the

    __signature__

    of the function.

|> Section
    *Compatible signatures for function operations.* 
    Taking the sum or product of functions etc
    presupposes some “compatibility” between their
    signatures—for 

    !! |> Pause
    !! *Constraints on function signatures imposed by
    !! function operations.* Taking the sum, product,
    !! quotient, etc, of two functions imposes certain
    !! constraints on their signatures, in order for the
    !! operation to not be physically meaningless.
    !! The following table shows the
    !! most general patterns that are possible:
    !! of signature possible when taking
    !! the sum, product, quotient, or composition of two functions:


|> Section
    work=ing
    *Function operation and units.* Taking the sum or
    product, etc, of two functions imposes certain
    constraints on their signatures in order for the operation
    to not be physically meaningless, in ways that are
    worth being aware of. The following table shows the
    most general pattern of signature possible when taking
    the sum, product, quotient, or composition of two functions:

    

    !! of two functions

    !! $$
    !! \Large (f + g)(x) = f(x) + g(x)
    !! $$
    $$
    \Large f + g
    $$

    !! presupposes that the inputs and outputs of
    !! $f$ and $g$ meet certain requirements; e.g.,

    !! $$
    !! \Large 
    !! $$

|> Section
    work=ing
    !! *Function operations.*
    *The constraints imposed by function operations
    on units.*
    When we add two functions the
    same input shows up as an input to both functions:

    $$
    \Large (f + g)(x) = f(x) + g(x)
    $$

    What this means is that if units are present 
    $f$ and $g$ must both take the _same kind_ of input, units-wise,
    in order for the function addition to
    make any sense; if we write

    $$
    \Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]
    $$

    !! to indicate that the inputs to $f$ have
    !! unit A and the output of $f$ have unit B, then
    to indicate that $f$ takes inputs with units A
    and produces outputs with units B, and

    $$
    \Large g : [\te{C}] \ra [\te{D}]
    $$

    likewise for $g$ for some units C and D, then the sum

    $$
    \Large f + g
    $$

    cannot/must not/should not take place unless

    $$
    \Large \te{A} = \te{C}
    $$

    because the same input $x$ must have units A
    when it is fed to $f$, and units C when it is fed
    to $g$! In addition, we must have
    !! the constraint
    !! to $g$!!!!!!!!!! Though in this case, the constraint

    $$
    \Large \te{B} = \te{D}
    $$

    also in this case, by the old addage that apples should
    not be added to oranges. (Specifically, note 
    that $f(x)$ has units B, that
    $g(x)$ has units D, and that $f(x)$ is added to $g(x)$.)
    
    By contrast,

    __apples can be multiplied by oranges__

    as testified by units such as __meters squared__ or __kilowatt-hours__
    or __worker years__ or even __meters per second__
    that is “meters times per seconds” (or “meters times seconds inverse ($\te{s}^{-1}$)”),
    !! (take _meters squared_ or _kilowatt-hours_,
    !! or even _meters per second_, where in the last
    !! case we multiply _meters_ by 
    !! _per seconds_, $\te{s}^{-1}$)
    !! and so 
    so to form the product of two functions

    $$
    \Large fg
    $$

    with signatures 

    $$
    \begin{gathered}
    \,\Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}], \\
    \Large g : [\te{C}] \ra [\te{D}] \up{1.35}
    \end{gathered}
    $$

    the constraint 

    $$
    \Large \te{A} = \te{C}
    $$

    must still be obeyed (because

    $$
    \Large (fg)(x) = f(x)g(x)
    $$

    and ‘$x$’ again appears as an input to both $f$ and
    $g$), but, by  the constraint

    $$
    \Large \te{B} = \te{D}
    $$

    can be dropped, because any kind of unit can be
    multiplied by any other kind of unit! So in this case
    the “general form” of $f$ and $g$ would be

    $$
    \begin{gathered}
    \,\Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}], \\
    \,\Large g : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{C}]\, \up{1.35}
    \end{gathered}
    $$

    for some units A, B, C, while in the case of function
    addition the “general form” would be

    $$
    \begin{gathered}
    \,\Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}], \\
    \,\Large g : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]\, \up{1.35}
    \end{gathered}
    $$

    with only two degrees of freedom in the units, A and B.

    !! $$
    !! \Large \te{A} = \te{C}
    !! $$

    !! remains, while the constraint

    !! $$
    !! \Large \te{B} = \te{D}
    !! $$
    
    !! is dropped,
    !! by contrast,
    !! and the output units B and D can be 
    !! completely arbitrary!

    !! is lost, for $f : [A] \ra [B]$, $g : [C] \ra [D]$.
    !! —because the same input $x$ is used as
    !! an input to both $f$ and $g$—but



    !! “seconds inverse”,
    !! or “one over seconds”, $\te{s}^{-1}$)
    !! must also be satisfied in order for the function
    !! addition to make any sense, because the outputs
    !! $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are added together, and by the
    !! old addage about adding apples to oranges.



    !! $$
    !! \begin{gathered}
    !! \Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}] \\
    !! \Large g : [\te{C}] \ra [\te{D}] \up{1.2}
    !! \end{gathered}
    !! $$

|> Section
    work=ing
    *The chain rule.* 
    Starting slow, we invite you to verify the following
    multiplication:

    $$
    2.22 \times 10.1 = 22.422.
    $$

    Here is a multiplication only
    of units:

    $$
    \,\left[{\te{m}\over \te{s}}\right]\times\left[{\te{\$}\over \te{m}}\right] = \left[{\te{\$}\over \te{m}}\right].
    $$

    We can combine the two:

    |> Boxed
        $$
        2.22[\te{m}/\te{s}] \times 10.1[\te{\$}/\te{m}] = 22.422[\te{\$}/\te{s}]
        $$

    The multiplication of

    __meters per second__

    (“[m$/$s]”) by

    __dollars per meter__

    (“[\$$/$m]”) gives

    __dollars per second__

    (“[\$$/$s]”) yay!
